course: dictionary
lesson: 4202
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, look at the words around it. «Every gorilla has its own pattern of nose wrinkles, like a fingerprint, so scientists can recognise each one from photos.» The clues are nose wrinkles, fingerprint and photos, so a gorilla here is an animal.
explain: «A silverback gorilla beats his chest with cupped hands, and the popping drum sound tells rivals how big he is.» Chest, drum sound and rivals all point to the animal. Always try your meaning in the sentence to check that it fits.
explain: Some people use gorilla in an informal way to mean a big, rough, frightening man. This is not kind to say. If you read it, look for clues such as rough or threatening, and remember that you should not call people names.
fact: A gorilla is the largest kind of ape, a very strong, mostly gentle animal with dark fur.
fact: Gorillas live in the forests of central Africa and mostly eat plants.
fact: Gorillas live in family groups, often led by a big male called a silverback.
fact: Gorillas are endangered, so they are protected.
fact: Informally, «gorilla» can mean a big, rough, frightening man, which is not kind to say.
exercise: «Every gorilla has its own pattern of nose wrinkles, like a fingerprint.» In this sentence, what is a gorilla? | A big, rough, frightening man | A place in a zoo | *An animal, the largest kind of ape | A kind of tree
exercise: «A silverback gorilla beats his chest, and the drum sound tells rivals how big he is.» Why does he do this? | To clean his fur | *To show rivals how big he is, often so a real fight is not needed | To show he is hungry | To call his family to dinner
exercise: «Dian Fossey studied mountain gorillas in Rwanda.» Where did she study them? | Keepers | Poachers | Dian | *Rwanda
test: «Koko the gorilla used many signs, but scientists still argue how much she understood.» What do scientists still disagree about? | *How much Koko understood | Where she lives | Whether she is a gorilla | How many signs she used
test: What do gorillas mostly eat? | Insects | *Plants | Fish | Meat
exam: «Scientists can recognise each gorilla from photos without catching it.» Why is this possible? | Photos are always taken very close up | Gorillas wear name tags | Gorillas stay still for every photo | *Each gorilla has its own pattern of nose wrinkles, like a fingerprint
